WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.780
How do you pull one microbe out of a crowded drop?

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.140
Soil, seawater, or a cow's rumen holds many kinds mixed together.

3
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.180
Under a microscope, many look alike, small with no nucleus, hiding what they can do.

4
00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:21.457
Instead of sorting by eye, you change the world inside the dish.

5
00:00:22.857 --> 00:00:27.537
Set conditions only one microbe can exploit, and let growth separate it from the

6
00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:30.477
rest. That is enrichment culture.

7
00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:37.037
You choose one nutrient and one oxygen level, so cells able to use them outgrow the

8
00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.037
rest.

9
00:00:38.417 --> 00:00:41.377
Change the recipe, and a different microbe wins.

10
00:00:42.637 --> 00:00:46.117
Make nitrogen gas, N2, the only nitrogen on offer.

11
00:00:47.457 --> 00:00:51.457
Only fixers, with the enzyme nitrogenase, can turn it into ammonia.

12
00:00:52.817 --> 00:00:58.377
Cut oxygen, and gut microbes ferment food by splitting bonds the host has no enzyme

13
00:00:58.377 --> 00:01:02.777
for. The goal is an isolate, one kind growing alone.

14
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.810
Yet the right recipe fails if you stop too soon.

15
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.530
Growth is set by the number of generations, not by the hours.

16
00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:17.410
The gut bacterium Escherichia coli finishes 9 doublings in 3 hours.

17
00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:22.890
A slow microbe called MK-D1 needs 20 days for one doubling.

18
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:28.190
Picture MK-D1 in a dish, needing those same 9 doublings.

19
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.910
Suppose the culture shows its reported lag phase, waiting 30 to 60 days before

20
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.630
growth starts. How many days would that dish need?

21
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:48.270
9 doublings times 20 days equals 180 days.

22
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.670
Add the 30 - to 60 day lag phase.

23
00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.230
That gives about 210 to 240 days.

24
00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.450
3 hours is a sliver of one 20-day doubling.

25
00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:06.830
So in 3 hours, MK-D1 grows only about 1.004 fold.

26
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.940
Stopping too soon is one trap, because slow growers look absent.

27
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.420
A second trap sits under the microscope.

28
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.180
Say a rumen microbe you cultured is small, with no nucleus.

29
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:27.120
A methanogen and the bacterium E. coli both fit, yet sit in different domains,

30
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:28.780
life's broadest groups.

31
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.380
Looks alone cannot tell bacterium from archaeon, so other tests are needed.

32
00:02:35.483 --> 00:02:39.383
Patient, selective dishes unlocked hidden players in nature.

33
00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:46.023
In nitrification, oxygen-requiring microbes oxidise ammonia through nitrite to

34
00:02:46.023 --> 00:02:48.083
nitrate by pulling away electrons.

35
00:02:49.543 --> 00:02:55.643
In 2005, Könneke and colleagues reported a marine isolate showing archaea oxidise

36
00:02:55.643 --> 00:02:57.703
ammonia alongside bacteria.

37
00:02:57.703 --> 00:03:04.603
And cultures of MK-D1 revealed its 20-day doubling time and long lag phase.

38
00:03:05.717 --> 00:03:11.157
Every recipe still carries a limit: a dish only catches microbes suited to your

39
00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:17.977
conditions. Low-oxygen sediments favour denitrification, reducing nitrate to gases,

40
00:03:18.457 --> 00:03:21.057
while oxygenated dishes favour nitrification.

41
00:03:22.457 --> 00:03:26.877
Match the recipe and the clock to the ability you seek, and the microbes sort

42
00:03:26.877 --> 00:03:27.297
themselves.
